    "['set protocols ldp transport-address [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls ldp router-id lo0 force', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'mpls ldp router-id lo0 force', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco command for the Juniper `set protocols ldp transport-address 1.1.1.1` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nmpls ldp router-id lo0 force\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if you specifically need to set the transport address (which in Cisco is typically handled by the router-ID):\\n\\n##\\nmpls ldp discovery transport-address 1.1.1.1\\n##\\n\\nNote: The first version using `router-id` is more common in Cisco configurations since it serves both as the LDP router ID and transport address, while the second version directly matches the transport address functionality. The loopback interface (lo0) should have the IP address 1.1.1.1 configured in the Cisco configuration.\", 'trans_template': 'mpls ldp router-id lo0 force'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls ldp discovery transport-address 1.1.1.1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'mpls ldp discovery transport-address 1.1.1.1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco command for the Juniper `set protocols ldp transport-address 1.1.1.1` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nmpls ldp router-id lo0 force\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, if you specifically need to set the transport address (which in Cisco is typically handled by the router-ID):\\n\\n##\\nmpls ldp discovery transport-address 1.1.1.1\\n##\\n\\nNote: The first version using `router-id` is more common in Cisco configurations since it serves both as the LDP router ID and transport address, while the second version directly matches the transport address functionality. The loopback interface (lo0) should have the IP address 1.1.1.1 configured in the Cisco configuration.\", 'trans_template': 'mpls ldp discovery transport-address 1.1.1.1'}]]": 1,
